{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Inference-time: same RSFT model with/without evidence. No-evi uses @2048; with-evi uses @1024 (1.7B: @2048). Tax $=$ no-evi $-$ with-evi; positive $=$ cost. Training-time tax in Appendix~\ref{sec:training_tax}.\par}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evidence Strategy Comparison}
\label{sec:evidence_strategy}

We compare joint generation against post-hoc BM25 retrieval (entity-constrained, $k{=}2$), using Evi-F1 (joint) with the DREEAM-protocol denominator~\citep{ma2023dreeam}. Post-hoc retrieval achieves higher Rel-F1 (48.76) by avoiding the evidence tax but lower Evi-F1 (joint) of 34.7 (EDCR 0.766). RSFT CED reaches 36.1 (EDCR 0.6487), a 1.4-point evidence advantage. CED reranking at inference (best-of-$N{=}8$ by CED score) achieves Rel-F1 48.64 and Evi-F1 81.88 (TP-only), approaching no-evidence extraction quality while preserving evidence.


\subsection{Generalization}
\label{sec:generalization}

Cross-architecture validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}) shows the largest RSFT gain (+5.45pp) with complete pairwise seed separation (\S\ref{sec:scaling}). The RSFT improvement transfers across datasets: on Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred}, RSFT achieves 41.45 Ign-F1 (+4.86pp over SFT 36.59), matching the DocRED gain (+5.08pp). Our best joint Evi-F1 (36.1, DREEAM-protocol) falls below supervised DREEAM (54.15\%~\citep{ma2023dreeam}), a gap driven by extraction performance rather than evidence quality (TP-only Evi-F1 exceeds 80\%). On the Re-DocRED test set, RSFT Ign-F1 drops approximately 20pp relative to dev across all evaluated scales (e.g., 14B: 57.9$\to$38.5), attributable to 2.82$\times$ higher annotation density in test (34.7 vs.\ 12.3 gold triples per document); model ranking is perfectly preserved (Spearman $\rho{=}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:test_results} in Appendix). Full results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:generalization_full}.


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_evidence_tax.pdf}
\vspace{-4mm}
\caption{Inference-time evidence tax across model scales (RSFT models, Re-DocRED test). Negative values indicate evidence \emph{benefits} extraction. Error bars show $\pm$1 std (all scales have $\geq$2 seeds).}
\label{fig:evidence_tax}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

EDCR (\S\ref{sec:edcr}) validates as a diagnostic at both cross-method ($\rho = -0.96$, $n = 7$, $p < 0.001$) and per-relation levels ($\rho = -0.79$, $n = 76$, $p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{sec:edcr_per_relation}). CED and flat-NLI produce comparable RSFT results (Rel-F1 45.63 vs.\ 45.61); stricter selection ($k{=}1$) outperforms $k{=}3$ by 1.02 Ign-F1 (Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}). RSFT reduces truncation FN, hallucinated FP, and duplicates by 6--22\% across scales, but evidence quality is unchanged ($\Delta < 0.4$pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:error_analysis}): RSFT improves \emph{what} the model extracts but not \emph{how well} it grounds extractions. Evidence generation adds 16--24\% wall-clock time at 4B--14B but reduces it at 1.7B by 13.6\%, as evidence constrains output structure (Appendix~\ref{sec:inference_efficiency}).

